HEVCProbe - can RISC OS reach the Raspberry Pi 4's HEVC decoder?
==================================================================

A first step towards hardware HEVC (H.265) decoding in Reel. The Pi 4,
Pi 400 and CM4 have an HEVC decoder block that the ARM drives directly
(Linux calls it "rpivid"). This program checks whether RISC OS can see it.

What it does, in order:
  1. asks the firmware which board this is (it stops unless it's a Pi 4,
     400 or CM4);
  2. asks about the HEVC clock: on or off, its rate and limits;
  3. maps the block's registers and reads its interrupt controller;
  4. turns the HEVC clock on at its top rate, reads the block's
     registers (VERSION is the interesting one), and puts the clock back
     as it was.

It only reads the block; the one thing it changes is the clock, and only
for a moment. The HEVC registers are only read with the clock on.

Running it:
  Double-click Probe. A window shows the report, which is also saved as
  Result in this directory. ProbeNoClock does steps 1-3 only: try it
  first if you'd rather, then Probe.

If the machine locks up at step 4 (it shouldn't: the registers are only
read with the clock confirmed on), switch it off and on again: nothing
is written to the block, so nothing is left behind.

Please send the Result file back.

Needs: RISC OS 5 on a Raspberry Pi 4, 400 or CM4, with BCMSupport (in
the ROM). Part of riscos-ffmpeg; GPL version 2 or later.
